{"module":"black-towns","voice":"female","duration":216.0,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"What we built: Black towns, institutions and self-determination","start":0.0,"end":4.67,"words":[[0.05,0.28],[0.28,0.44],[0.44,0.83],[1.1,1.41],[1.41,1.88],[2.15,3.09],[3.09,3.32],[3.32,4.57]]},{"el":"intro","text":"This history is not only about what was done to Black Americans.","start":5.47,"end":9.48,"words":[[5.53,5.73],[5.73,6.22],[6.22,6.35],[6.35,6.56],[6.56,6.91],[6.91,7.25],[7.25,7.46],[7.46,7.66],[7.66,7.87],[7.87,8.01],[8.01,8.28],[8.28,8.97]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Within a generation of slavery, Black communities built towns, banks, schools and businesses from almost nothing.","start":9.83,"end":17.59,"words":[[9.87,10.23],[10.23,10.37],[10.37,11.15],[11.15,11.29],[11.29,11.93],[12.18,12.47],[12.47,13.18],[13.18,13.46],[13.46,13.89],[14.13,14.56],[14.81,15.16],[15.16,15.38],[15.38,15.94],[15.94,16.23],[16.23,16.73],[16.73,17.15]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Knowing what was built explains what was lost, and shows the model for rebuilding.","start":17.94,"end":23.08,"words":[[18.0,18.36],[18.36,18.58],[18.58,18.8],[18.8,19.09],[19.09,19.74],[19.74,19.96],[19.96,20.18],[20.18,20.54],[20.8,21.02],[21.02,21.31],[21.31,21.45],[21.45,21.82],[21.82,21.96],[21.96,22.61]]},{"el":"h0","text":"All-Black towns","start":23.43,"end":25.01,"words":[[23.48,24.23],[24.23,24.86]]},{"el":"s0","text":"After Reconstruction, Black families founded their own towns where they could own land, vote and govern themselves.","start":25.71,"end":32.47,"words":[[25.76,26.03],[26.03,26.98],[27.21,27.49],[27.49,27.96],[27.96,28.43],[28.43,28.63],[28.63,28.84],[28.84,29.17],[29.17,29.38],[29.38,29.58],[29.58,29.78],[29.78,29.99],[29.99,30.32],[30.56,30.83],[30.83,31.03],[31.03,31.37],[31.37,31.98]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Oklahoma alone had more than 50, the most of any state, including Boley (founded 1903).","start":32.82,"end":40.01,"words":[[32.87,33.63],[33.63,34.01],[34.01,34.24],[34.24,34.4],[34.4,34.62],[34.62,35.08],[35.35,35.5],[35.5,35.88],[35.88,36.04],[36.04,36.27],[36.27,36.72],[36.99,37.6],[37.6,37.98],[37.98,38.52],[38.52,39.51]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Others include Nicodemus, Kansas (1877, now a National Historic Site); Eatonville, Florida (1887, one of the first incorporated Black towns and Zora Neale Hurston's hometown); Mound Bayou, Mississippi (1887, founded by Isaiah Montgomery, who had been enslaved); and Allensworth, California (1908, founded by Colonel Allen Allensworth, now a state historic park).","start":40.36,"end":66.69,"words":[[40.41,40.69],[40.69,41.11],[41.11,41.82],[42.07,42.49],[42.49,43.77],[44.02,44.23],[44.23,44.37],[44.37,44.87],[44.87,45.43],[45.43,45.79],[46.03,46.6],[46.85,47.34],[47.34,48.41],[48.65,48.87],[48.87,49.01],[49.01,49.15],[49.15,49.43],[49.43,50.28],[50.28,50.57],[50.57,50.92],[50.92,51.13],[51.13,51.42],[51.42,51.63],[51.63,52.12],[52.12,52.76],[53.01,53.36],[53.36,53.72],[53.96,54.53],[54.53,55.59],[55.84,56.34],[56.34,56.55],[56.55,56.97],[56.97,57.75],[58.0,58.14],[58.14,58.35],[58.35,58.57],[58.57,59.2],[59.45,59.66],[59.66,60.3],[60.55,61.19],[61.19,62.11],[62.35,62.85],[62.85,63.06],[63.06,63.42],[63.42,63.7],[63.7,64.34],[64.58,64.8],[64.8,64.94],[64.94,65.29],[65.29,65.86],[65.86,66.14]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Black Wall Streets","start":67.59,"end":69.4,"words":[[67.64,68.13],[68.13,68.51],[68.51,69.25]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Greenwood in Tulsa was the most famous, but not the only one.","start":70.1,"end":74.35,"words":[[70.15,70.65],[70.65,70.8],[70.8,71.16],[71.16,71.38],[71.38,71.52],[71.52,71.88],[71.88,72.39],[72.64,72.86],[72.86,73.07],[73.07,73.22],[73.22,73.58],[73.58,73.87]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Durham's Parrish Street was home to North Carolina Mutual Life Insurance (1898), for decades the largest Black-owned business in America.","start":74.7,"end":84.57,"words":[[74.75,75.21],[75.21,75.6],[75.6,75.99],[75.99,76.23],[76.23,76.54],[76.54,76.69],[76.69,76.93],[76.93,77.63],[77.63,78.25],[78.25,78.56],[78.56,79.26],[79.26,80.51],[80.78,80.93],[80.93,81.48],[81.48,81.63],[81.63,82.18],[82.18,82.8],[82.8,83.27],[83.27,83.42],[83.42,84.05]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Richmond's Jackson Ward, Maggie L. Walker founded the St. Luke Penny Savings Bank in 1903, the first bank chartered by a woman in the United States.","start":84.92,"end":95.25,"words":[[84.97,85.1],[85.1,85.72],[85.72,86.21],[86.21,86.48],[86.72,87.0],[87.0,87.21],[87.55,87.83],[87.83,88.31],[88.31,88.45],[88.45,88.79],[89.14,89.34],[89.34,89.62],[89.62,90.1],[90.1,90.38],[90.38,90.52],[90.52,91.41],[91.65,91.79],[91.79,92.07],[92.07,92.34],[92.34,92.76],[92.76,92.96],[92.96,93.1],[93.1,93.45],[93.45,93.59],[93.59,93.72],[93.72,94.28],[94.28,94.76]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Atlanta's Auburn Avenue had Atlanta Life Insurance, founded by Alonzo Herndon, who was born enslaved.","start":95.6,"end":102.33,"words":[[95.65,96.2],[96.2,96.48],[96.48,96.9],[96.9,97.11],[97.11,97.6],[97.6,97.87],[97.87,98.57],[98.81,99.3],[99.3,99.51],[99.51,99.99],[99.99,100.48],[100.72,100.86],[100.86,101.07],[101.07,101.28],[101.28,101.91]]},{"el":"s1","text":"(See the module on communities destroyed.)","start":102.68,"end":105.4,"words":[[102.71,102.86],[102.86,103.01],[103.01,103.44],[103.44,103.59],[103.59,104.32],[104.32,104.98]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Institutions that lasted","start":106.3,"end":108.3,"words":[[106.35,107.41],[107.41,107.67],[107.67,108.2]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Free Black Philadelphians formed the Free African Society, a mutual-aid group, in 1787, and Richard Allen founded the African Methodist Episcopal Church in 1816.","start":109.0,"end":122.31,"words":[[109.01,109.25],[109.25,109.57],[109.57,110.54],[110.54,110.87],[110.87,111.03],[111.03,111.27],[111.27,111.84],[111.84,112.57],[112.85,113.01],[113.01,113.9],[113.9,114.31],[114.59,114.75],[114.75,116.21],[116.49,116.73],[116.73,117.22],[117.22,117.54],[117.54,118.11],[118.11,118.27],[118.27,118.84],[118.84,119.48],[119.48,120.21],[120.21,120.62],[120.62,120.78],[120.78,121.83]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The first Black newspaper, Freedom's Journal, began in 1827; the Chicago Defender later helped drive the Great Migration.","start":122.66,"end":131.67,"words":[[122.71,122.86],[122.86,123.15],[123.15,123.44],[123.44,124.16],[124.41,124.92],[124.92,125.42],[125.68,126.04],[126.04,126.18],[126.18,127.41],[127.67,127.81],[127.81,128.32],[128.32,128.82],[128.82,129.19],[129.19,129.55],[129.55,129.91],[129.91,130.05],[130.05,130.42],[130.42,131.21]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Cheyney University (1837) is the oldest HBCU.","start":132.02,"end":136.65,"words":[[132.07,132.51],[132.51,133.24],[133.24,134.26],[134.52,134.67],[134.67,134.81],[134.81,135.32],[135.32,136.13]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Negro Motorist Green Book (1936–1966) guided Black travelers to safe hotels, restaurants and gas stations in a country full of sundown towns.","start":137.0,"end":148.79,"words":[[137.05,137.21],[137.21,137.66],[137.66,138.34],[138.34,138.64],[138.64,138.87],[138.87,141.44],[141.7,142.16],[142.16,142.46],[142.46,143.06],[143.06,143.21],[143.21,143.52],[143.52,144.12],[144.39,145.07],[145.07,145.29],[145.29,145.52],[145.52,146.12],[146.12,146.28],[146.28,146.43],[146.43,146.88],[146.88,147.11],[147.11,147.26],[147.26,147.79],[147.79,148.24]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Organizing for themselves","start":149.69,"end":151.69,"words":[[149.74,150.56],[150.56,150.74],[150.74,151.47]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Ida B. Wells documented lynching and urged Black families to leave Memphis and buy guns for self-defense.","start":152.39,"end":159.1,"words":[[152.44,152.72],[152.72,152.94],[153.3,153.58],[153.58,154.37],[154.37,154.87],[154.87,155.09],[155.09,155.38],[155.38,155.66],[155.66,156.16],[156.16,156.31],[156.31,156.52],[156.52,156.95],[156.95,157.17],[157.17,157.38],[157.38,157.67],[157.67,157.81],[157.81,158.6]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Fannie Lou Hamer founded the Freedom Farm Cooperative in 1969 so Black families in Mississippi could grow their own food and own land.","start":159.45,"end":168.19,"words":[[159.46,159.76],[159.76,159.91],[159.91,160.28],[160.28,160.8],[160.8,160.95],[160.95,161.4],[161.4,161.63],[161.63,162.45],[162.45,162.6],[162.6,163.87],[163.87,164.1],[164.1,164.4],[164.4,164.92],[164.92,165.07],[165.07,165.67],[165.67,165.9],[165.9,166.19],[166.19,166.42],[166.42,166.64],[166.64,166.87],[166.87,167.09],[167.09,167.32],[167.32,167.69]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Black Panther Party's Free Breakfast for Children program (1969) fed thousands of children every day and is often credited with pushing the federal government to expand school breakfasts.","start":168.54,"end":181.27,"words":[[168.59,168.74],[168.74,169.05],[169.05,169.43],[169.43,169.81],[169.81,170.04],[170.04,170.65],[170.65,170.8],[170.8,171.42],[171.42,172.03],[172.03,173.33],[173.59,173.82],[173.82,174.43],[174.43,174.59],[174.59,175.2],[175.2,175.5],[175.5,175.73],[175.73,175.96],[175.96,176.12],[176.12,176.42],[176.42,177.03],[177.03,177.26],[177.26,177.64],[177.64,177.8],[177.8,178.33],[178.33,179.02],[179.02,179.17],[179.17,179.71],[179.71,180.01],[180.01,180.78]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Why it matters now","start":182.17,"end":183.63,"words":[[182.22,182.51],[182.51,182.7],[182.7,183.18],[183.18,183.47]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Many of these places were attacked precisely because they succeeded (see the modules on communities destroyed and on evidence of performance, where economist Lisa Cook shows how violence suppressed Black invention).","start":184.33,"end":196.71,"words":[[184.38,184.67],[184.67,184.82],[184.82,185.04],[185.04,185.57],[185.57,185.71],[185.71,186.09],[186.09,186.76],[186.76,187.13],[187.13,187.35],[187.35,187.94],[187.94,188.09],[188.09,188.24],[188.24,188.76],[188.76,188.91],[188.91,189.66],[189.66,190.25],[190.25,190.47],[190.47,190.62],[190.62,191.14],[191.14,191.29],[191.29,191.96],[192.22,192.44],[192.44,193.11],[193.11,193.41],[193.41,193.63],[193.63,193.93],[193.93,194.15],[194.15,194.75],[194.75,195.27],[195.27,195.57],[195.57,196.24]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The model they used still works: own land, bank together, build institutions, and teach the next generation.","start":197.06,"end":203.73,"words":[[197.1,197.23],[197.23,197.54],[197.54,197.73],[197.73,197.98],[197.98,198.23],[198.23,198.54],[198.76,198.95],[198.95,199.26],[199.48,199.73],[199.73,200.17],[200.39,200.64],[200.64,201.45],[201.67,201.86],[201.86,202.11],[202.11,202.23],[202.23,202.55],[202.55,203.3]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Black Americans founded more than 130 banks between 1888 and 1934; only about 20 remain today.","start":204.08,"end":212.56,"words":[[204.13,204.41],[204.41,205.04],[205.04,205.53],[205.53,205.67],[205.67,205.88],[205.88,207.0],[207.0,207.35],[207.35,207.77],[207.77,208.69],[208.69,208.9],[208.9,209.88],[210.12,210.47],[210.47,210.83],[210.83,211.25],[211.25,211.67],[211.67,212.09]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Supporting them is one of the quests.","start":212.91,"end":215.06,"words":[[212.96,213.42],[213.42,213.61],[213.61,213.74],[213.74,213.93],[213.93,214.06],[214.06,214.19],[214.19,214.65]]}]}